# Dixon City Council Meeting Summary — October 5, 2026

The Dixon City Council held a brief regular session Monday evening, with City Manager Danny Langloss Jr. absent due to a conference. All four council members and Mayor Glen Hughes were present.

## Domestic Violence Awareness Month

Mayor Hughes opened with a proclamation declaring October 2026 as Domestic Violence Awareness Month, presenting it to a representative from the YWCA. Hughes acknowledged the difficult nature of the subject while stressing public awareness.

## Claims and Minutes

The council approved minutes from the September 21 meeting and authorized payment of $1,513,971.02 in labor and outside claims. The largest single item — $1,100,599 — was a payment on the wastewater treatment plant improvement project.

## Sister City Visit to Germany

Mayor Hughes reported on a recent visit to Dixon's sister city of Herzberg, Germany, a relationship dating to 1999. Among the highlights: members of the visiting group helped paint a mural on a community building in Herzberg, completed in just two days. Hughes noted the sister city organization handles most of its own costs through fundraising, with the city contributing about $6,000 for the most recent visit.

## Council Member Reports

**Councilman Chris Bishop** noted a new mural is nearing completion on the Water Department building, with a dedication expected the week of October 13.

**Councilman Mary Oros** recognized Justin Bergman, director of the Small Business Development Center at Sauk Valley Community College, who received the Illinois State Star Award. Oros encouraged local business owners to take advantage of the center's free services.

**Councilman Mike Venier** highlighted several items:
- **Bunnell Industries** held a ribbon-cutting for a new 100,000-square-foot manufacturing facility, also celebrating 66 years in business as a four-generation family operation with 109 employees.
- A **golf cart tour of the new trail** across the Petunia Crossing Bridge is scheduled for October 13, 9 a.m. to 1 p.m., starting at the Emma Hubs Tennis Court facility. The tour is aimed at seniors and others unable to walk the trail. A coffee truck will be on site.
- The **Ultimate Tailgate** event (Bears vs. Packers, ribs and chili cook-off) is this Sunday in the Bean Blossom parking lot.
- **Scarecrow Fest** is October 17.
- Dixon's **200th anniversary** is approaching in a couple of years, and the municipal band is working on a commemorative song. Venier urged the council to begin planning.

**Councilman Dennis Considine** had no report.

## Liquor Code Amendment for Ultimate Tailgate

The council unanimously approved a temporary liquor code amendment to allow alcohol service in the Bean Blossom parking lot and adjacent alley for the Ultimate Tailgate event, per a request from the Dixon Chamber of Commerce and Dixon Main Street.

## Chronic Nuisance Property Ordinance Overhaul

City Attorney Robert LeSage III presented a substantially revised chronic nuisance property ordinance — the first major update in roughly 30 years. The revision was developed in coordination with Police Chief Ryan Bivins, Deputy Chief Aaron Simonton, and city management following concerns raised at the previous council meeting.

Key changes:
- **Qualifying conditions expanded** from a short list to 19 factors, now including noise violations, additional criminal code provisions, and serious property maintenance violations.
- **Repeat occurrences of the same violation** now count toward the threshold. Previously, three *different* factors had to be present — a standard so high the ordinance was rarely if ever used. Now, three separate instances of the same type of violation (e.g., drug activity on three distinct occasions) can trigger the ordinance.
- **The look-back period** was extended from 90 to 120 days.
- **Due process protections** were strengthened, including better notice requirements and the option for property owners to enter settlement agreements or abatement plans.
- **Judicial remedies were broadened.** The old ordinance mandated a minimum 30-day shuttering of a property. Judges now have a full range of equitable remedies, which LeSage said should make courts more willing to enforce the ordinance.

The council approved the changes unanimously. LeSage noted the police department will need to develop new forms before putting properties through the revised process.

## Zoning Referral

The council voted unanimously to refer a petition for a zoning amendment on behalf of Robertson Construction for Lot 15 of Liberty Court to the Plan Commission for review.

The meeting adjourned after approximately 30 minutes with no public comment.

